Using a virtual CanoKey with libvirt / virt-manager
===================================================

libvirt has no native CanoKey device, so it is attached with libvirt's QEMU
command-line passthrough. This package provides the directory
/var/lib/libvirt/qemu/canokey/ for the state files. Confined QEMU processes can
use it: the SELinux policy already allows it, and this package adds the
AppArmor rule.

Edit the domain (virsh edit <vm>, or the XML tab in virt-manager):

1. Add the QEMU namespace to the <domain> element:

   <domain type='kvm' xmlns:qemu='http://libvirt.org/schemas/domain/qemu/1.0'>

2. Add, just before </domain>:

   <qemu:commandline>
     <qemu:arg value='-device'/>
     <qemu:arg value='{"driver":"canokey","id":"canokey0","file":"/var/lib/libvirt/qemu/canokey/VMNAME.canokey","bus":"usb.0"}'/>
   </qemu:commandline>

   Replace VMNAME. The file name must end in .canokey. The file is created on
   the first start of the guest and keeps the key state (FIDO2 credentials,
   PINs, OpenPGP and PIV keys). Delete it to get a factory-new key.

Notes:

- Use the JSON syntax shown above. libvirt creates its own devices with JSON
  -device options, which QEMU processes after plain "-device canokey,..."
  options, so the plain form fails with "Bus 'usb.0' not found".
- The guest needs a USB controller with the alias "usb" (the default qemu-xhci
  controller created by virt-manager has it).
- The virtual key reports user presence without a touch, so it cannot be used
  to test touch prompts.
- This is a testing tool: the key material is stored unencrypted in the state
  file, and all guests running as the qemu user can access every state file in
  the directory.
- State files created by canokey-qemu builds before 2026 (libcanokey-qemu.so.0)
  can be opened, but credentials registered with those builds no longer work:
  neither FIDO2 resident credentials nor the non-resident credentials used by
  e.g. systemd-cryptenroll are recognized. Re-enroll them, or start with a new
  state file.
